+
+Read the numbers: on 1,000,000 USDT the real Aave pool paid **30,547.86 USDT** over a
+year. `sharePrice()` did not move by one unit. The sole depositor withdrew
+**997,000 USDT** — her principal minus the 0.30% withdrawal fee, and none of the
+interest. **33,547.86 USDT** was left behind at an address with no function that can
+move it.
+
+The same defect stated as a stateful invariant, falsified in two calls — see A-04 for
+the output.
+
+### Fix
+
+Make `totalAssets()` read reality instead of a cached number:
+
+```solidity
+function totalAssets() public view returns (uint256) {
+    return aUsdt.balanceOf(address(this));
+}
+```
+
+aUSDT is rebasing — `balanceOf` already includes accrued interest — so this alone makes
+yield flow to shareholders through the existing `convertToShares` / `convertToAssets`
+maths. Then delete the `totalAssetsStored` state variable and every `+=` / `-=` on it;
+keeping both is how the two diverged in the first place.
+
+`withdraw()` must also stop passing a stale figure to Aave: compute
+`grossAssets = convertToAssets(shares)` *after* the switch (so it includes yield),
+and burn the shares before the external call, which it already does.
+
+Whichever way you go, add a test that asserts `totalAssets()` tracks
+`aUsdt.balanceOf(address(this))` — that single assertion would have caught this.
